Autumn 1923. At half past nine they found Amos Reed, the lock-keeper, drowned in his own lock. It looks like a fall from the gate in the dark. You are the county constable. The coroner comes at three. By then you need a case, or it goes down as an accident.

Towpath

A cinder path along the canal. The cottage door is west, the lock north, the lock hut south. Water knocks against the lock gate.

Exits: west, north, south.

> w
w

Kitchen

The cottage kitchen, low and warm, with a black iron stove. The door to the towpath is east, the parlour west, the stairs go up.

A pair of town boots dries by the stove. Hester Reed, Amos's daughter, is here.

Exits: east, west, up.

> examine boots
examine boots

Town boots, wet to the ankle, with canal mud in the welts. Marsh said he never left the parlour.

You write it in your notebook: Marsh was out in the wet.

> e
e

Towpath

A cinder path along the canal. The cottage door is west, the lock north, the lock hut south. Somewhere a rope creaks.

Bert Pike, the lock hand, is here.

Exits: west, north, south.

> s
s

Lock Hut

A brick hut for the lock hands. Oilskins on a nail, a tool chest under the window.

The tool chest contains a coil of rope.

Exits: north.

Bert Pike comes in.

> search chest
search chest

You find a windlass.

You write it in your notebook: the weapon.

